title: Daniel 11 – A Prophetic Overview
description: Daniel 11 verse by verse, from the Persian kings to Antiochus IV Epiphanes, matched to history from 536 to 164 BC, and how it points ahead to the Antichrist.
output: lessons/prophecy/daniel-11.html

# Rebuilt on the deck template from the original Daniel 11 slides (a design-tool export), same acts and same order.
# Verses are the full KJV text of each passage. The history was checked, and a few dates and names corrected;
# the final act is marked where the history stops matching Antiochus.

=== Introduction
--- Daniel 11 [title]
overline: Bible Study
sub: A Prophetic Overview · 536–164 B.C. Four centuries of history, foretold in precise detail nearly 400 years before it came to pass.
refs: Daniel 11:1-4; Daniel 11:36
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- Why this chapter astonishes
kicker: Prophecy written before the history
lead: Daniel 11 bridges the silent centuries between the close of the Old Testament and the time of Christ, tracing the Persian Empire, Greece, and the wars of the North and South.
stat: ~400 | years of silence between the last Old Testament prophet and the time of Christ
stat: ~536 B.C. | when Daniel received it, in the third year of Cyrus
stat: ~370 | years foretold, from the Persian kings down to Antiochus IV (536 to 164 B.C.)
callout: The conclusion: only an omniscient God could dictate such precise historical realities hundreds of years before they occurred. [[Daniel 10:1]]

--- The sweep of Daniel 11
kicker: Narrative order, not to scale
tile: 539 | Darius the Mede
tile: 480 | Persia attacks Greece
tile: 323 | Alexander’s empire divided
tile: 274 | North and South begin to fight
tile: 198 | The North takes Israel
tile: 175 | Antiochus IV rises
tile: 167 | The temple is desecrated
tile: 164 | Antiochus IV dies

--- The King of the North and the King of the South
panel: King of the North | The Seleucid Empire: Syria and Mesopotamia. Capital: Antioch.
panel: King of the South | The Ptolemaic Empire: Egypt. Capital: Alexandria.
callout: Between them lay Israel, “the glorious land.” Daniel 11 traces the long tug-of-war of these two dynasties, with Israel caught in the middle. [[Daniel 11:16]]

=== Act I: Persia and Greece
--- Persia and the Rise of Greece [title]
overline: Act I · Daniel 11:1-4
sub: From the kings of Persia to a mighty Greek king whose empire is shattered the moment it peaks.

--- Three kings of Persia, and a rich fourth
kicker: Act I · ~530–465 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:1-2
p: **History.** After Cyrus came Cambyses II, Smerdis and Darius I. The fourth, Xerxes I, was far richer than all of them, and he hurled his armies against Greece in the campaign of Thermopylae and Salamis (480 B.C.).

--- Alexander’s empire, split four ways
kicker: Act I · ~336–323 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:3-4
p: **History.** Alexander the Great conquered widely and died young in 323 B.C. His empire was divided among four of his generals, toward the four winds of heaven.
panel: Ptolemy, the South | Egypt. His line drives Daniel 11.
panel: Seleucus, the North | Syria and Mesopotamia. His line drives Daniel 11.
panel: Lysimachus | Asia Minor and Thrace.
panel: Cassander | Macedonia and Greece.

=== Act II: North versus South
--- The Wars of North and South [title]
overline: Act II · Daniel 11:5-20
sub: Political marriages, broken alliances, and armies surging back and forth across the glorious land.

--- The first alliance, and its betrayal
kicker: Act II · ~252 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:5-6
p: **History.** Berenice, daughter of Ptolemy II of Egypt, marries Antiochus II of Syria to seal a peace. After her father’s death the arrangement fails, and she is abandoned and later murdered.

--- Berenice avenged
kicker: Act II · ~246–241 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:7-8
p: **History.** Her brother Ptolemy III invades the North, defeats Seleucus II, and returns to Egypt with captives and treasures, including idols.

--- The sons strike back
kicker: Act II · ~225–219 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:9-10
p: **History.** The sons of Seleucus II, Seleucus III and Antiochus III the Great, assemble great forces to retaliate against Egypt.

--- A prideful victory
kicker: Act II · ~217 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:11-12
p: **History.** Ptolemy IV defeats Antiochus III at Raphia, but his proud victory does not strengthen him.

--- The North returns in force
kicker: Act II · ~200 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:13-15
p: **History.** Antiochus III returns with a larger army, and Egypt’s enemies join him against Ptolemy V. He captures fortified cities, including Sidon. Some Jews who tried to help him establish the vision fell short.

--- Into the glorious land
kicker: Act II · ~198–192 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:16-17
p: **History.** Antiochus III defeats Ptolemy V and takes Israel, then gives him his daughter Cleopatra I in marriage. She sides with Egypt, and his plan fails.

--- Stopped by Rome, and gone
kicker: Act II · ~190–175 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:18-20
p: **History.** Antiochus III is beaten by Rome at Magnesia (Lucius Scipio) and dies in 187 B.C. His successor, Seleucus IV, taxes heavily and is dead within a few years, not in battle and not in open anger.

=== Act III: Antiochus IV
--- Antiochus IV Epiphanes [title]
overline: Act III · Daniel 11:21-35
sub: A vile person who seizes power by flattery, desecrates the temple, and foreshadows the Antichrist to come.

--- A prophecy with two fulfillments
lead: Daniel 11 is fulfilled in two layers. Antiochus IV fulfills it first, in the second century before Christ. The same words reach their final fulfillment in the Antichrist at the time of the end, and the end is what matters most.
panel: First fulfillment | Antiochus IV Epiphanes, 175–164 B.C. His flatteries, his war on the covenant and the desecration of the temple are history, and the Maccabees lived through them.
panel-warn: Final fulfillment | The Antichrist, at the time of the end. Jesus speaks of the abomination of desolation as still to come, long after Antiochus. [[Matthew 24:15]] [[2 Thessalonians 2:3-4]] [[Daniel 12:1]]
callout: Antiochus is the pattern and the Antichrist is the fulfillment. Watch how the verses fit the first, and then reach past it to the last.

--- A vile person rises
kicker: Act III · ~175–171 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:21-22
p: **History.** Antiochus IV seizes power by flattery, overwhelms his opponents, and has the high priest Onias III killed, the “prince of the covenant.”

--- Strong through deceit
kicker: Act III · ~170 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:23-24
p: **History.** He makes deceptive alliances, grows strong with a small following, and lavishes plunder on them as he moves against strongholds.

--- War against the South
kicker: Act III · ~170–169 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:25-26
p: **History.** Antiochus IV defeats Ptolemy VI in battle, helped by betrayal within Egypt’s own court.

--- Lies at one table
kicker: Act III · ~169 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:27-28
p: **History.** Antiochus and Ptolemy VI talk peace and lie to one another, and neither plan succeeds. Antiochus returns home with great riches, plundering the temple on the way, with his heart set against Israel’s religion.

--- Turned back by Rome’s ships
kicker: Act III · ~168 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:29-30
p: **History.** His second campaign in Egypt is halted when the Roman envoy Popilius Laenas, with Rome’s ships behind him, orders him out. Furious, he turns on Israel and favors the Jews who forsook the covenant.

--- The abomination of desolation
kicker: Act III · ~167 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:31
p: **History.** Antiochus’ forces and apostate Jews defile the temple, stop the daily sacrifice, and set up an altar to Zeus on the altar of the Lord, the “abomination that maketh desolate.” Jesus speaks of it again as still to come. [[Matthew 24:15]]

--- The people who know their God
kicker: Act III · ~167–164 B.C.
scripture: Daniel 11:32-35
p: **History.** Faithful Jews resist and teach others, the Maccabean revolt follows, and many are persecuted and martyred, refined for the time of the end.

=== Act IV: The willful king
--- The Willful King and the End [title]
overline: Act IV · Daniel 11:36-45
sub: He magnifies himself above all, and comes to his end with none to help him.

--- He magnifies himself above all
kicker: Act IV · Daniel 11:36-37
scripture: Daniel 11:36-37
p: **First fulfillment.** Antiochus IV took the title Epiphanes, “God manifest,” and exalted himself above the gods of his fathers.
p: **Final fulfillment.** The same words describe the man of sin who exalts himself above all that is called God. [[2 Thessalonians 2:3-4]]

--- The god of forces
kicker: Act IV · Daniel 11:38-39
scripture: Daniel 11:38-39
p: **First fulfillment.** Antiochus honored a god his fathers did not know, read as Zeus Olympios, and rewarded his followers with gold and silver and precious stones, dividing the land among them for gain.
p: **Final fulfillment.** A world ruler who honors a god of power and rewards those who follow him.

--- The time of the end
kicker: Act IV · Daniel 11:40-43
scripture: Daniel 11:40-43
p: **First fulfillment.** Only in part. Rome turned Antiochus back from Egypt in 168 B.C., and nothing in the record has him ruling Egypt, Libya and Ethiopia.
p: **Final fulfillment.** These verses reach to the time of the end, as the chapter itself says, when the king of the north comes like a whirlwind.

--- He comes to his end
kicker: Act IV · Daniel 11:44-45
scripture: Daniel 11:44-45
p: **First fulfillment.** Antiochus died in Persia in 164 B.C., not in the holy mountain.
p: **Final fulfillment.** The palace planted between the seas in the glorious holy mountain, and an end with none to help him, belong to the Antichrist, and Daniel 12 goes on at once to the time of trouble. [[Daniel 12:1]]

=== Conclusion
--- History foretold, and yet to come
panel: First fulfillment | Detailed history from the Persian kings to Antiochus’ desecration of the temple, fulfilled between 536 and 164 B.C.
panel: Final fulfillment | The end is what matters most: a future Antichrist, the abomination of desolation, and persecution, as in Daniel 12.
panel: Undeniable | The precision of biblical prophecy gives us faith in God’s word, and undeniable proof of its truth.
read: daniel-8-12-timeline | Daniel 8–12 Timeline
